
		============================
		** BRINGING UP THE SYSTEM **
		============================




INTRODUCTION
------------

    The following intructions should be followed when installing
an XCOMP ST/S or ST/R controller with a Rotating Memory Systems
RMS-506/512 hard disk drive.




INSTALLATION
------------

1. Set the controller I/O address so that it does not conflict
   with any other I/O in your computer. See the XCOMP RMS Users
   Manual for proper switch setting. Be sure to set switch 7 to
   the OFF position. This is in conflict with the manual. The
   RMS drive has a fast step capability which is not supported
   by a Seagate Drive (for which the manual was written).

2. Change the equate statement CBASE in all programs to reflect
   the I/O address used in Step #1. Assemble all the programs
   using the Digital Research macro assembler MAC.

3. Install the controller in your system. Do so with power off!

4. Connect the short 26 pin ribbon cable between the two boards.

5. Connect both the 34 & the 20 pin cables from the controller
   to the drive. Pin 1 of both cables on the controller are 
   connected to Pin 1 on the drive connectors. Pin 1 is denoted
   by a triangle on the connectors and by the red wire on the
   ribbon cable.

6. Plug the power cable into the drive. Do so with power off.




TESTING
-------

Run the following programs in sequence:

1. XSBT. This will test the buffer memory of the controller. It also
   ensures proper I/O address decoding and the ability of the host
   processor to transfer data to/from the controller.

2. RMSATS. Tests the step logic of both the controller & the drive.
   Also ensures that the control cable (34 pin) is installed
   correctly.

3. RMSFMT. Formats the drive. This needs to be done before data can
   be written onto the drive. Tests the ability of both the controller
   & the drive to read & write data. Ensures that the data cable (20 pin)
   has been installed correctly. Note that this program overwrites previous
   data.

4. RMSRDT. Random read/write test. Good test to verify that the complete
   sub-system (including drive power supply) is working properly. Note
   that this test destroys any previous data on the disk.

5. At this point you are ready to bring up CP/M on your hard disk.
